WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.571
Verb Tense and Verb Forms

00:00:02.951 --> 00:00:06.976
Match the time clues. Every sentence needs one main verb.

00:00:08.176 --> 00:00:09.266
How to spot it

00:00:09.666 --> 00:00:11.611
One verb in different tenses

00:00:12.161 --> 00:00:14.313
Time clues like dates and “since”

00:00:14.863 --> 00:00:18.578
It’s testing: the verb tense the timeline calls for

00:00:19.778 --> 00:00:20.544
The method

00:00:20.944 --> 00:00:22.979
Step 1: Hunt for time clues

00:00:23.259 --> 00:00:27.005
Dates, “since”, “by the time”, other verbs.

00:00:27.555 --> 00:00:30.058
Step 2: Pick the time frame

00:00:30.338 --> 00:00:33.463
Finished past, past-to-now, or earlier past?

00:00:34.013 --> 00:00:36.137
Step 3: Check for a main verb

00:00:36.417 --> 00:00:41.574
Missing? Use a real verb. Already there? Use -ing or “to”.

00:00:42.124 --> 00:00:45.227
Step 4: Keep lists in one tense

00:00:45.507 --> 00:00:47.214
Same tense all the way through.

00:00:47.764 --> 00:00:48.858
In a few words

00:00:49.008 --> 00:00:51.903
Clue. Time frame. Main verb.

00:00:53.103 --> 00:00:53.885
The rules

00:00:54.285 --> 00:00:56.383
A past date = simple past

00:00:56.663 --> 00:01:01.129
Wrong: In 1928, Fleming has noticed the mold.

00:01:01.409 --> 00:01:05.626
Right: In 1928, Fleming noticed the mold.

00:01:06.176 --> 00:01:07.838
Since = has / have

00:01:08.118 --> 00:01:12.461
Wrong: Since 1945, the UN expanded.

00:01:12.741 --> 00:01:17.234
Right: Since 1945, the UN has expanded.

00:01:17.784 --> 00:01:19.445
Earlier past = had

00:01:19.725 --> 00:01:23.343
Right: By 1519, the Aztecs had built a huge city.

00:01:23.893 --> 00:01:25.702
One main verb per sentence

00:01:25.982 --> 00:01:28.948
Wrong: The study, which surveyed adults, finding that…

00:01:29.228 --> 00:01:32.114
Right: The study, which surveyed adults, found that…

00:01:32.664 --> 00:01:34.568
Authors argue in the present

00:01:34.848 --> 00:01:37.362
Right: Ruiz argues that… She shows…

00:01:38.562 --> 00:01:39.623
Let’s try one

00:01:40.023 --> 00:01:46.758
Before the Erie Canal opened in 1825, shipping a ton of flour from Buffalo to New York City took

00:01:46.758 --> 00:01:52.097
about three weeks. By the time the canal ______ its first full year of operation,

00:01:52.097 --> 00:01:54.249
the same trip took about eight days.

00:01:54.899 --> 00:02:00.691
Time clues: 1825, took, and by the time. It’s all in the past.

00:02:01.241 --> 00:02:05.012
Completes is present. Will complete is future.

00:02:05.012 --> 00:02:08.588
Has completed connects to now, not to the 1820s.

00:02:09.138 --> 00:02:13.634
Had completed means finished before a point in the past. Choice C.

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:15.903
Common traps

00:02:16.303 --> 00:02:17.560
Has vs had

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.932
“Has” connects to now. “Had” points to an earlier past.

00:02:22.482 --> 00:02:23.942
The -ing fragment

00:02:24.222 --> 00:02:26.331
“Finding” can’t be the main verb.

00:02:27.531 --> 00:02:29.502
Verb Tense and Verb Forms

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:32.676
Clue. Time frame. Main verb.

00:02:33.226 --> 00:02:33.806
Spot it

00:02:34.006 --> 00:02:36.909
One verb in different tenses + time clues

00:02:37.459 --> 00:02:38.166
Steps

00:02:38.366 --> 00:02:39.993
1. Hunt for time clues

00:02:40.273 --> 00:02:41.829
2. Pick the time frame

00:02:42.109 --> 00:02:43.972
3. Check for one main verb

00:02:44.252 --> 00:02:46.186
4. Keep lists in one tense

00:02:46.736 --> 00:02:47.460
Remember

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:50.655
Past date → simple past (noticed)

00:02:50.935 --> 00:02:52.600
since → has / have

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.542
Earlier past → had

00:02:54.822 --> 00:02:56.601
-ing can’t be the main verb

00:02:56.881 --> 00:02:59.137
Authors argue → present tense

00:02:59.687 --> 00:03:00.128
Trap

00:03:00.328 --> 00:03:03.291
has (up to now) vs had (earlier past)

00:03:04.041 --> 00:03:07.459
